test_entry -- the LaGriT entry point (Nov 2026)
=======================================================================

QUICK START

  sh run_entry_tests.sh [path-to-del_vor]   23 cases, about 2 s
  open summary.html

WHAT IS TESTED

  del_vor_lagrit_entry (src/del_vor_entry.F90), the routine LaGriT's
  dump / control_volume will call (phase 6c), with the mesh object's
  own arrays (used in place, never copied or changed) and the options
  as one string. The harness del_vor_entry_test (built by CMake next to
  del_vor; source in harness/) reads a mesh with del_vor's reader and
  calls the entry point through call_entry.f: fixed-form Fortran
  compiled with LaGriT's gfortran flags (-fdefault-integer-8
  -std=legacy ...), with no interface block, as LaGriT will.

    del_vor_entry_test MESH vol.txt [-sink] [-tamper WHAT] CALL ...

  one call per CALL string, in one process. -sink sends every log line
  through del_vor_log's hook (printed as "lagrit| line"; LaGriT's
  writloga will take its place).

  Each case of cases.txt runs, in work/NAME,
    prog/   del_vor -avs MESH ARGUMENTS     (the program, if any)
    entry/  the harness with the case's calls

  Cases that must work (tri2d ... twice): every output format, every
  cv, the ParaView and matrix files, a near-tie mesh (violations file),
  a non-Delaunay mesh with cv median, check_only, the options in
  LaGriT's keyword, value / form, no hook, and two calls in one
  process. Cases that must fail cleanly (bad_option ... tamper_nen):
  bad options, avs given, errors found inside the run (not Delaunay,
  failed mesh checks), parent/child chains (itp1 = 41), and LaGriT
  arrays not in the layout del_vor takes (a hex among triangles,
  itetoff not nen*(cell-1), a vertex number past nnodes, nen 5).

WHAT IS CHECKED (check_entry.py)

  ierr      each call returns the ierr cases.txt expects, and nothing
            stops (the harness exits 0, or 3 when its last call
            returned an error)
  message   the expected text is in the output
  program   the program ran with exit 0
  files     the entry point wrote the same files as the program; in
            the error cases, none at all
  bytes     every file equal byte for byte, apart from the .stor's line
            2 (date; the command) and the report's options lines (the
            program names its mesh file)
  line2     the .stor's line 2 ends with (del_vor OPTIONS)
  screen    the last call's lines equal the program's screen output;
            with -sink every line came through the hook
  volumes   the volumes returned equal the .stor's, every digit; none
            with check_only on (the caller's array is left as it was)

  The meshes of not_delaunay, flip_median and near_tie come from
  test_delaunay's generator, those of bad_mesh and parent from
  test_validity's (run into work/gen_d, work/gen_v).

PICTURES (logs/)

  NAME.svg  the mesh (a large tet mesh by its boundary faces), its
            vertices colored by the control volume the entry point
            returned (gray when none came back); red rings on parent
            vertices, cell 1 red when the harness spoiled it

FILES

  run_entry_tests.sh       runner (work/, logs/)
  cases.txt                the cases
  check_entry.py           checks, pictures, summary.html
  harness/                 del_vor_entry_test.F90, call_entry.f
